Sessions vs JWTs
.common methods for verifying a user's identity and control access to applications 
    .session-based authentication - stores user session data on the server and uses a session ID to identify authenticated users 
        .unique session to each user after a successful login 
        .server stores session data, client stores only a session ID(usually )
    .JSON-web authentication - uses a signed token stored on the client, allowing users to authenticate wothout maintaining server-side sessions
        .token-based authentication mechanism used to securely transmit user information between a client and server 
        .contains header, payload, and signature, allowing the server to verify the token without storing session data 
            .after a successful login, the server generates a JWT, which the client sends with future requests for authentication
            .they are stateless, making them suitable for scalable and distributed applications without maintaining server-side sessions 


    .when to use session vs JWT
        .depends on app architecture, scalability requirements, and security levels

        .traditional websites - SBA
        .banking and financial apps - SBA
        .content mgt systems - SBA
        .enterprise intranet apps - SBA
        .REST APIs - JWT
        .single page apps - JWT
        .mobile apps - JWT
        .microservices architecture - JWT
        .cross-domain authentication - JWT
        .cloud-native and distributed apps - JWT 


    

Access token VS refresh token
    .OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect use tokens instead of usernames and passwords to authenticate users and authorize access
    .access and refresh are the most common tokens often implemented as JWTs
        .access token - short-lived credential, that allows users to access protected resources without logging in repeatedly 
        .used to authenticate API requests and access protected resources
        .user authorizes the client, which receives an access token and uses it to access protected resources 
        .server validates the token before granting access
        .typical lifespan: 30 -90 minutes or a few hours 

        .refresh tokens - long-lived tokens used to obtain a new access token after the current one expires, without requiring the user to login again 
        .used to obtain a new access token after the current one expires without requiring the user to login again 
        .typical lifespan: much longer than hours, 90 days up to 



Tokens vs Cookies
    .both used to manage user authentication, but differ on how they are stored, transmitted, and secured 

    Token:
        .used for authentication and authorization, especially in APIs
        .sent manually in the authorization header
        .typically stored in memory, local storage, or session storage 
        .commonly interpreted as JWTs 
        .more vulnerable to XSS if stored insecurely

    Cookie:
        .used primarily for session mgt in web mgt
        .automatically sent by the browser with each request
        .stored and managed by the browser
        .can store session IDs or authentication tokens such as JWTs
        .more vulnerable to CSRF unless protected with SameSite or CSRF tokens 



JWT Validation 
.ensures that a token is authentic, has not been tampered with, and is still valid 
    .JWT:
        .header - contains metadata, such as the signing algorithm
        .payload - contains claims, i.e user and token information
        .signature - verifies the token's integrity using a secret or private key

    .during validation: 
        .verify the signature to ensure the token has not been modified
        .check the exp and nbf claims to confirm the token is valid
        .optionally validate the issuer and audience claims for additional security 


JWT Signing Algorithms 
    .use signing algorithms to ensure their integrity and authenticity

JWT signing keys
    .used to create and verify token signatures 
        .symmetric - uses the same secret key for signing and verification
        .assymetric - uses a private key for signing and a public key for verification 
            .more secure for distributed systems where multiple services need to verify tokens


Refresh token security
    .secure storage - store refresh tokens in HttpOnly cookies or other secure storage
    .expiration - set an expiration time and invalidate expired refresh tokens
    .token rotation - issue a new refresh token each time the old one is used and invalidate the previous one
    .revocation - allow refresh tokens to be revoked e.g on user logout or account compromise
    .concurrent use - restrict or monitor refresh token usage across multiple devices or sessions 




Role-based access control  
    .security framework used to restrict and manage user access to systems, applications, DBs, and network resources based on predefined job roles
        .instead of assigning permissions to every individual user, organizations assign permissions to roles and users inherit access rights through those roles 
            .supports security compliance and auditing req
            .reduces unauthorized access and insider threats
            .controls access to sensitive data, applications, and systems
            .assigns permissions according to job responsibilities
            .simplifies permission mgt in large organizations


    Components
        .users - individuals with accounts that access the system
        .roles - represent job function within an organization
        .permissions - define the actions a role is allowed to perform 
        .role assignments - users are assigned roles depending on their responsibilities 



    Working of RBAC
    .define roles 
    .assign permissions to role
    .assign users to roles
    .access control enforcement
    .modify roles and permissions
    .audit and monitor access




    Limitations
    .initial role setup is time-consuming
    .complex organizations may require many roles
    .poor role design can create permission overlap
    .frequent organizational changes may require continuous updates 


    






Open Authorization 
    .open-standard authorization protocol  that allows applications to access user data without requiring the user's password
    .it enables secure delegated access - login with facebook 
        .prevents sharing passwords with third-party apps
        .uses access tokens issued by an authorization server
        .works over HTTP/HTTPS and supports web, mobile, and desktop apps 


   Why its used 
    .to avoid sharing passwords with every app
    .to allow safe, controlled access to user data
    .to enable convenient, single-click login and authorization
    .to support modern applications across the web, mobile, and APIs


Components of the OAuth Mechanism 

.OAuth Provider - Authorization Server
    .verifies the user's identity and issues access/refresh tokens
    .stores and protects user accounts and data
    .handles login(authentication) 
    .asks the user for permission - consent screen
    .issues tokens used by applications to access APIs
        .e.g google, fb, github

OAuth Client - third party application
    .requests access to the users data from the provider
    .redirects the user to the provider for the login
    .never sees the user's password
    .




Resource Owner - User
    .the individual who owns the data and grants permission to share it 
        .approves or denies access through the consent screen
        .controls what information  the client  can access
        .can revoke permissions anytime from account settings
        .does not share their password with the OAuth Client





